title: How a run is batched
tags: Sync
kind: reference
summary: A run works through its links in batches — at most commit-batch-size links, fewer when they hold transfer-batch-size copies — each committed on its own, so a crash costs one batch.

A batch takes links in order (parents first) until it holds `commit-batch-size`
links, or until those links hold `transfer-batch-size` `copy` operations, whichever
comes first. Each batch runs its metadata, then its files, then one
`POST …/links/commit` ([[Running a sync]]). A crash in the middle of a batch leaves
its links uncommitted: the next plan finds them, and redoes what it must — the
transfers being the slow part, they bound the batch.

Both sizes come from the intents file's `[settings]` ([[The intents file]]): 50 and
20 by default. The plan records them — and the host chosen for the sync database —
in a metarecord of the plan repository with no `plan_kind`, since the run does not
read the intents file. A plan from before they were recorded runs with the
defaults.

The defaults were not measured ([[Sync open questions]]).
